      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both ACE inhibitors and are equally recommended as top first-line treatments for high blood pressure, but recent large studies suggest ARBs are preferred because they cause fewer side effects.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "ACE inhibitors",
            "link": "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/HYPERTENSIONAHA.120.16667"
          },
          {
            "link": "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2784663"
          }
        ],
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Both ACE inhibitors and are equally recommended as top first-line treatments for high blood pressure, but recent large studies suggest ARBs are preferred because they cause fewer side effects"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "How They Work"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both drug classes lower blood pressure by targeting your body's renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (), a hormone network that controls blood vessel width and fluid balance.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "link": "https://www.goodrx.com/conditions/hypertension/ace-inhibitors-vs-arbs-whats-the-difference"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "ACE Inhibitors stop your body from making angiotensin II, a natural chemical that narrows your blood vessels."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "(Angiotensin Receptor Blockers) let your body make that chemical, but they block the receptor sites so the chemical cannot tighten your blood vessels."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Differences in Side Effects"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "While both lower blood pressure and protect your heart and kidneys equally well, their side effect profiles differ:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "ACE Inhibitors: Often end in \"-pril\" (such as lisinopril or enalapril). They frequently cause a stubborn, dry cough and can rarely cause dangerous deep skin swelling called angioedema. This happens because they increase a protein called bradykinin."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "ARBs: Usually end in \"-sartan\" (such as losartan or valsartan). They do not affect bradykinin levels, meaning patients rarely get a dry cough or angioedema."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Shared Warnings"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Pregnancy: Neither drug class should be used during pregnancy because they can harm a developing baby."
          },
          {
            "snippet": ": Both can raise potassium levels in your blood, requiring basic blood tests after starting treatment."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Combination: Doctors generally do not prescribe an ACE inhibitor and an ARB together because it increases the risk of kidney damage and low blood pressure without extra benefit."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Are you looking into these for yourself or someone else ?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Are you managing any other conditions (like kidney disease or diabetes)?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can share more specific details on dosing or other blood pressure medications."
      }
    ],
